window.MAIS={"problems": [{"id": "MAIS-O1", "n": 1, "title": "Quantitative bounded Löb", "file": "MAIS-O1.md", "safety": ["cooperative AI", "agent foundations"], "math": ["logic", "complexity theory"], "stars": 3, "empirical": false, "resolved": false, "agenda": "A1", "short": "Löb's theorem converts a proof of \"if $P$ is provable then $P$\" into a proof of $P$. The conversion is an explicit construction, so it has a cost, measured in symbols of proof. That cost is what a transparent software agent pays to conclude cooperation from its opponent's conditional promise of cooperation, so quantifying it would turn the Löbian argument from a possibility theorem into a budget."}, {"id": "MAIS-O2", "n": 2, "title": "Recovering world-models from behavior", "file": "MAIS-O2.md", "safety": ["interpretability"], "math": ["statistics", "probability", "complexity theory"], "stars": 2, "empirical": false, "resolved": false, "agenda": "A2", "short": "Tomography reconstructs the inside of a body from measurements taken outside it. Here the body is an agent, the measurements are its actions, and the object to reconstruct is the causal model its behavior relies on. Richens and Everitt [RE24] proved the analogue of Radon's inversion formula: a policy family that stays near-optimal across all local interventions on its environment determines an approximate causal model of that environment."}, {"id": "MAIS-O3", "n": 3, "title": "The geometry and identifiability of superposition", "file": "MAIS-O3.md", "safety": ["interpretability"], "math": ["statistics", "optimization", "probability"], "stars": 3, "empirical": false, "resolved": false, "agenda": "A3", "short": "The working tool for reading concepts out of a neural network is the sparse autoencoder, an $\\ell^1$-penalized dictionary learner. Every safety audit built on it inherits the answer to a mathematical question: when does the estimator return the network's true feature directions, and when does it return artifacts — two co-occurring concepts fused into one direction, the feature absorption documented empirically by Chanin et al. [CWDB+24]?"}, {"id": "MAIS-O4", "n": 4, "title": "Training for interpretability", "file": "MAIS-O4.md", "safety": ["interpretability"], "math": ["optimization", "convex geometry", "probability"], "stars": 3, "empirical": false, "resolved": false, "agenda": "A4", "short": "A network asked to store more features than it has dimensions must let them interfere. Post-hoc interpretability deciphers whatever storage scheme training happened to invent; the complementary problem is to train networks whose features are legible in the first place, and to know what that legibility costs in performance."}, {"id": "MAIS-O5", "n": 5, "title": "Representation theory of learned circuits", "file": "MAIS-O5.md", "safety": ["interpretability"], "math": ["dynamical systems", "probability", "representation theory"], "stars": 3, "empirical": false, "resolved": false, "agenda": "A5", "short": "Train a small network to multiply in $S_5$ and read off which irreducible representations its weights use. Chughtai, Chan, and Nanda [CCN23] did this four times from four random initializations and got four answers: {sign, standard, standard⊗sign, a 5-dimensional}; {sign, standard}; {sign, standard, the other 5-dimensional}; {sign, standard}."}, {"id": "MAIS-O6", "n": 6, "title": "Does geometric simplicity force a legible mechanism?", "file": "MAIS-O6.md", "safety": ["interpretability", "generalization"], "math": ["algebraic geometry", "harmonic analysis"], "stars": 3, "empirical": false, "resolved": false, "agenda": "A6", "short": "A small network trained to add residues mod $p$, when reverse-engineered by Nanda, Chan, Lieberum, Smith, and Steinhardt [NCLSS23], turns out to be running a discrete Fourier algorithm: each hidden neuron carries a single frequency. Is that mechanism an accident of training, or is it forced by the geometry of the loss?"}, {"id": "MAIS-O7", "n": 7, "title": "Opposing staircases", "file": "MAIS-O7.md", "safety": ["generalization"], "math": ["algebraic geometry"], "stars": 2, "empirical": false, "resolved": false, "agenda": "A7", "short": "Gradient flow on a deep linear network descends a staircase: it lingers at a saddle where only the $k$ strongest modes of the target are fitted, then drops to the next. Watanabe's free-energy asymptotics assign a complexity invariant to each stage — the local learning coefficient $\\lambda$ of Lau, Furman, Wang, Murfet, and Wei [LFWMW23], which controls generalization on the Bayesian side."}, {"id": "MAIS-O8", "n": 8, "title": "A predictive theory of out-of-distribution generalization", "file": "MAIS-O8.md", "safety": ["generalization"], "math": ["probability", "optimization", "dynamical systems"], "stars": 3, "empirical": false, "resolved": false, "agenda": "A8", "short": "In 2022, Langosco et al. [LKSP+22] trained a reinforcement-learning agent to collect a coin in a side-scrolling video game. In every training level the coin sat at the far right end; when the experimenters moved it, the agent ran right past it to the end of the level. It had learned \"move right,\" not \"get the coin\" — two policies that agree on every training level, so the data could not distinguish them. Yet training reliably produced one and not the other."}, {"id": "MAIS-O9", "n": 9, "title": "Steering Conway's Life from a small seed", "file": "MAIS-O9.md", "safety": ["control"], "math": ["probability", "dynamical systems", "complexity theory"], "stars": 3, "empirical": false, "resolved": false, "agenda": null, "short": "Can a small controller reliably steer a vast system to a desired global state, despite noise it cannot remove and rival processes it did not design? This is an abstraction of a core difficulty of AI alignment: goal-directed control of a complex world from limited leverage, with the difficulty concentrated where it is in practice — robustness and competition."}, {"id": "MAIS-O10", "n": 10, "title": "Polynomial overhead for Löb's theorem", "file": "MAIS-O10.md", "safety": ["cooperative AI", "agent foundations"], "math": ["logic", "complexity theory"], "stars": 2, "empirical": false, "resolved": false, "agenda": "A1", "short": "How much can proving $\\Box P \\to P$ save over proving $P$ directly? If the answer is \"only polynomially much,\" then every proof budget in the Löbian cooperation story of Critch [C19] — the thresholds at which source-reading agents establish each other's cooperation — becomes polynomially effective. This page states the expected answer; deciding it is the problem."}, {"id": "MAIS-O11", "n": 11, "title": "Does Löb's rule shorten proofs?", "file": "MAIS-O11.md", "safety": ["cooperative AI", "agent foundations"], "math": ["logic", "complexity theory"], "stars": 3, "empirical": false, "resolved": false, "agenda": "A1", "short": "Löb's rule says: to prove $P$, prove $\\Box P \\to P$ instead. Does the detour ever save a single symbol? Not one example is known. The stakes are in Löbian cooperation, where bounded agents read each other's source code and cooperate exactly when they find a proof of the other's cooperation within a fixed symbol budget (MAIS-A1); without lower bounds on how long such proofs must be, those budgets rest on heuristics alone."}, {"id": "MAIS-O12", "n": 12, "title": "Expansion and diagonalization costs in Peano arithmetic", "file": "MAIS-O12.md", "safety": ["cooperative AI", "agent foundations"], "math": ["logic", "complexity theory"], "stars": 2, "empirical": false, "resolved": false, "agenda": "A1", "short": "If you hold an $m$-symbol proof of $\\varphi$ in your hand, how many symbols does it take to prove the sentence \"$\\varphi$ has an $m$-symbol proof\"? Every quantitative Löbian argument — the overhead of Löb's theorem, the FairBot cooperation threshold — reduces to two such system constants, and neither has been determined for any concrete system."}, {"id": "MAIS-O13", "n": 13, "title": "Provable comparisons between exp-log terms in arithmetic", "file": "MAIS-O13.md", "safety": ["cooperative AI", "agent foundations"], "math": ["logic"], "stars": 2, "empirical": false, "resolved": false, "agenda": "A1", "short": "Proof-based agents cooperate via Löb's theorem; agents with bounded compute need Critch's parametric bounded Löb theorem [C19], in which \"provable\" becomes \"provable within $f(k)$ symbols\": if $S$ proves, uniformly in $k$, that budgeted provability of $p(k)$ implies $p(k)$, and the budget $f$ grows fast enough, then $S$ proves $p(k)$ outright for all sufficiently large $k$."}, {"id": "MAIS-O14", "n": 14, "title": "Bounded Löb at logarithmic proof budgets", "file": "MAIS-O14.md", "safety": ["cooperative AI", "agent foundations"], "math": ["logic"], "stars": 3, "empirical": false, "resolved": false, "agenda": "A1", "short": "The parametric bounded Löb theorem, due to Critch [C19], extends Löb's theorem to proofs of bounded length; it is the engine of Löbian cooperation, in which proof-based agents that can read one another's source code prove their way to mutual cooperation within a stated proof budget. For which budgets does it hold?"}, {"id": "MAIS-O15", "n": 15, "title": "Explicit thresholds in parametric bounded Löb", "file": "MAIS-O15.md", "safety": ["cooperative AI", "agent foundations"], "math": ["logic", "complexity theory"], "stars": 2, "empirical": false, "resolved": false, "agenda": "A1", "short": "The parametric bounded Löb theorem is due to Critch [C19]; the form fixed here is its repair by GPT 5.6 Sol [S26]. It is an existence theorem: given a proof of the reflection scheme $\\forall k\\ (\\Box_{f(k)}\\ p(k) \\to p(k))$, it produces some threshold $\\hat{k}$ above which $p(k)$ is provable. An agent allocating a proof-search budget needs the threshold itself. How does $\\hat{k}$ depend on the data?"}, {"id": "MAIS-O16", "n": 16, "title": "The FairBot cooperation threshold", "file": "MAIS-O16.md", "safety": ["cooperative AI"], "math": ["logic", "computational"], "stars": 2, "empirical": false, "resolved": false, "agenda": "A1", "short": "AI systems are software agents, and programs, unlike people, can exhibit their source code cheaply. Whether transparent agents can cooperate where opaque ones defect is a basic question of cooperative AI — and in the simplest case it has been reduced to a single unknown number."}, {"id": "MAIS-O17", "n": 17, "title": "Cooperation thresholds over families of bounded agents", "file": "MAIS-O17.md", "safety": ["cooperative AI"], "math": ["logic", "complexity theory"], "stars": 2, "empirical": false, "resolved": false, "agenda": "A1", "short": "Two copies of Critch's $\\mathrm{FairBot}_k$ [C19] — the agent that searches all proofs of at most $k$ symbols for a proof that its opponent cooperates against it, cooperating if the search succeeds and defecting otherwise — cooperate in the Prisoner's Dilemma once the proof budget $k$ passes a threshold. That is one point of a landscape: over all pairs of transparent agents of bounded description length, how fast does the cooperation threshold grow?"}, {"id": "MAIS-O18", "n": 18, "title": "Admissible budgets for a bounded Löb axiom", "file": "MAIS-O18.md", "safety": ["cooperative AI", "agent foundations"], "math": ["logic"], "stars": 3, "empirical": false, "resolved": false, "agenda": "A1", "short": "Löb's theorem has two strengths. The rule — from $\\vdash \\Box P \\to P$ infer $\\vdash P$ — is what the parametric bounded Löb theorem of Critch [C19] quantifies, and it is the engine of Löbian cooperation: agents with bounded compute cooperating because each finds a short proof about the other's behavior."}, {"id": "MAIS-O19", "n": 19, "title": "Soundness of Gödel–Löb logic under polynomial budgets", "file": "MAIS-O19.md", "safety": ["cooperative AI", "agent foundations"], "math": ["logic"], "stars": 3, "empirical": false, "resolved": false, "agenda": "A1", "short": "Solovay [S76] proved that Gödel–Löb logic $\\mathsf{GL}$ — propositional modal logic with the distribution axiom $\\Box(A \\to B) \\to (\\Box A \\to \\Box B)$, the Löb axiom $\\Box(\\Box A \\to A) \\to \\Box A$, and the necessitation rule — captures exactly the propositional laws of provability in Peano arithmetic."}, {"id": "MAIS-O20", "n": 20, "title": "A bounded Payor lemma and its cooperation threshold", "file": "MAIS-O20.md", "safety": ["cooperative AI", "agent foundations"], "math": ["logic"], "stars": 2, "empirical": false, "resolved": false, "agenda": "A1", "short": "Löb's theorem has a rival. Payor's lemma — proved by James Payor and written up by Critch [C23] — says: if $S \\vdash \\Box(\\Box\\varphi \\to \\varphi) \\to \\varphi$, then $S \\vdash \\varphi$ — and its four-line proof uses neither the diagonal lemma nor inner necessitation."}, {"id": "MAIS-O21", "n": 21, "title": "Is the Löb overhead invariant under polynomial simulation?", "file": "MAIS-O21.md", "safety": ["cooperative AI", "agent foundations"], "math": ["logic", "complexity theory"], "stars": 2, "empirical": false, "resolved": false, "agenda": "A1", "short": "The Löb overhead $F_S(k,n)$ — the least proof length of the hardest-to-prove sentence $P$ of length at most $n$ whose reflection instance $\\Box P \\to P$ has a proof of at most $k$ symbols, the box being the ordinary unbounded provability predicate \"$P$ is provable in $S$\" — depends on the proof calculus, the numeral system, the abbreviation discipline, and the Gödel coding."}, {"id": "MAIS-O22", "n": 22, "title": "A bounded analogue of Solovay's completeness theorem", "file": "MAIS-O22.md", "safety": ["cooperative AI", "agent foundations"], "math": ["logic"], "stars": 3, "empirical": false, "resolved": false, "agenda": "A1", "short": "Proof-based agents cooperate by searching for proofs about one another's behavior; a bounded agent can search only within a proof-length budget, so which provability laws survive bounding determines which Löbian cooperation arguments it can run (MAIS-A1). Solovay's theorem [S76] pins down the unbounded laws: Gödel–Löb logic $\\mathsf{GL}$ proves a modal formula if and only if every arithmetical realization of it is a theorem of Peano arithmetic."}, {"id": "MAIS-O23", "n": 23, "title": "Do margins imply behavioral identifiability of causal models?", "file": "MAIS-O23.md", "safety": ["interpretability"], "math": ["algebraic geometry", "probability"], "stars": 3, "empirical": false, "resolved": false, "agenda": "A2", "short": "If two agents act optimally in every locally tampered version of an environment, masked observations included, must they hold the same causal model of it? Richens and Everitt [RE24] answered yes for Lebesgue-almost-every parameter choice, and world-model discovery from behavior rests on this. But the exceptional null set is described only implicitly, as the vanishing locus of polynomials arising in their proof, and an auditor cannot check membership in an unnamed null set."}, {"id": "MAIS-O24", "n": 24, "title": "Explicit polynomial margins replacing generic causal identifiability", "file": "MAIS-O24.md", "safety": ["interpretability"], "math": ["algebraic geometry", "probability", "complexity theory"], "stars": 2, "empirical": false, "resolved": false, "agenda": "A2", "short": "The theorem behind behavioral world-model discovery is due to Richens and Everitt [RE24]: near-optimal adaptation to all local interventions and observation masks determines the agent's causal model. Their theorem holds for almost every parameter choice, with the exceptions described only as the vanishing locus of polynomials arising in the proof."}, {"id": "MAIS-O25", "n": 25, "title": "Query complexity of causal model extraction from optimal policies", "file": "MAIS-O25.md", "safety": ["interpretability"], "math": ["complexity theory", "statistics"], "stars": 2, "empirical": false, "resolved": false, "agenda": "A2", "short": "Each question to an optimal agent — what do you do in this tampered environment? — returns a single bit. Mixing two interventions with weight $q$ and bisecting on the weight at which the agent's action switches converts those bits into binary digits of an interventional probability, and sweeping over interventions recovers the agent's whole causal model. That is the algorithm of Richens and Everitt [RE24], run with unlimited exact queries."}, {"id": "MAIS-O26", "n": 26, "title": "Bisection attains optimal query complexity for causal model extraction", "file": "MAIS-O26.md", "safety": ["interpretability"], "math": ["complexity theory", "statistics"], "stars": 2, "empirical": false, "resolved": false, "agenda": "A2", "short": "How many yes-or-no questions does it take to read a causal model out of an optimal agent's behavior? The answer prices behavioral interpretability: the number of experiments an auditor must spend to learn what a black-box agent believes about its world. Information theory sets the floor: a model with $K$ free table entries, each to be recovered to accuracy $\\varepsilon$, costs $\\Omega(K\\log(1/\\varepsilon))$ queries by a packing argument (the agenda's Remark 3.3)."}, {"id": "MAIS-O27", "n": 27, "title": "How agent regret limits causal model identifiability", "file": "MAIS-O27.md", "safety": ["interpretability"], "math": ["statistics", "probability"], "stars": 3, "empirical": false, "resolved": false, "agenda": "A2", "short": "An analyst reading a causal model out of an agent's behavior faces two costs of different kinds: sampling error, which more queries repair, and the agent's own imperfection, which no budget repairs. A policy family of regret $\\delta$ can be shared by distinct models, and the analyst can never tell those models apart. This problem asks for the exact size of that irreducible ambiguity."}, {"id": "MAIS-O28", "n": 28, "title": "Causal model identifiability under average-case regret", "file": "MAIS-O28.md", "safety": ["interpretability"], "math": ["probability", "statistics"], "stars": 2, "empirical": false, "resolved": false, "agenda": "A2", "short": "Extraction theorems ground a safety hope: that an evaluator with only behavioral access can recover the causal world model an agent's competence presupposes."}, {"id": "MAIS-O29", "n": 29, "title": "Estimation rates and query design for Boltzmann agents", "file": "MAIS-O29.md", "safety": ["interpretability"], "math": ["statistics", "optimization"], "stars": 2, "empirical": false, "resolved": false, "agenda": "A2", "short": "Between the optimal agent (noiseless bits) and the adversarially $\\delta$-suboptimal agent (irreducible ambiguity) sits the standard model of a noisily rational one: the Boltzmann, or quantal-response, agent, which plays better actions exponentially more often. Recovering a causal model from its sampled actions is a smooth statistics problem rather than an adversarial one — and it comes with a design question: which experiments should the analyst run?"}, {"id": "MAIS-O30", "n": 30, "title": "Which interventions identify which parts of the causal graph?", "file": "MAIS-O30.md", "safety": ["interpretability"], "math": ["combinatorics", "probability"], "stars": 3, "empirical": false, "resolved": false, "agenda": "A2", "short": "Richens and Everitt [RE24] proved that a causal model of the environment can be read off an agent's optimal behavior, and their proof supplies an algorithm. That algorithm anchors itself with hard interventions on every environment variable at once. A real experimenter can usually tamper with only a few. Which arrows of the graph, and which probability tables, can still be read off the agent's behavior when the intervenable set shrinks?"}, {"id": "MAIS-O31", "n": 31, "title": "What one intervenable variable reveals about a causal chain", "file": "MAIS-O31.md", "safety": ["interpretability"], "math": ["probability"], "stars": 2, "empirical": false, "resolved": false, "agenda": "A2", "short": "Take the simplest causal structure there is, a chain $C_m\\to C_{m-1}\\to\\dots\\to C_1$ of binary variables, and an agent paid to guess the far end $C_1$ while observing nothing. An analyst may tamper with the chain at a single link $C_j$ and watch how the agent's guess responds. How much of the chain's probability law does that one dial recover?"}, {"id": "MAIS-O32", "n": 32, "title": "Tightness of the 1/n rate for depth-n goal agents", "file": "MAIS-O32.md", "safety": ["interpretability"], "math": ["probability", "combinatorics"], "stars": 2, "empirical": false, "resolved": false, "agenda": "A2", "short": "A theorem of Richens, Abel, Bellot, and Everitt [RABE25] extracts an environment's transition probabilities from an agent that achieves multi-step goals, using no interventions at all: an optimal agent's first action, on goals of the form \"achieve this transition at most $k$ times in $n$ tries, or else more than $k$ times,\" reveals binomial medians, and sweeping $k$ pins each transition probability to a window of width $O(1/n)$, where $n$ is the depth of the goals the agent can handle."}, {"id": "MAIS-O33", "n": 33, "title": "Tolerable corruption fraction for goal-based model extraction", "file": "MAIS-O33.md", "safety": ["interpretability"], "math": ["complexity theory", "combinatorics", "probability"], "stars": 3, "empirical": false, "resolved": false, "agenda": "A2", "short": "An agent whose behavior reveals its world-model may have stable blind spots: a fixed fraction of (state, goal) pairs on which its answers are arbitrary — the same wrong answers every time, not fresh noise. Channel noise is dispatched by repetition at logarithmic cost; persistent corruption cannot be, since re-asking a corrupted question returns the same lie — the regime of lie-tolerant binary search studied by Rivest, Meyer, Kleitman, Winklmann, and Spencer [RMKWS80]."}, {"id": "MAIS-O34", "n": 34, "title": "Exact identified set for two-variable causal models", "file": "MAIS-O34.md", "safety": ["interpretability"], "math": ["algebraic geometry", "computational"], "stars": 1, "empirical": false, "resolved": false, "agenda": "A2", "short": "The smallest environment supporting a causal question has two binary variables and one arrow: does $X$ cause $Y$ or $Y$ cause $X$? An agent is paid a known function of its action and both variables, observes nothing, and acts near-optimally under every mixture of local interventions. Which models of the environment are consistent with its behavior?"}, {"id": "MAIS-O35", "n": 35, "title": "Finite-sample recovery of two-variable causal models", "file": "MAIS-O35.md", "safety": ["interpretability"], "math": ["statistics", "probability"], "stars": 1, "empirical": false, "resolved": false, "agenda": "A2", "short": "Radon's inversion formula became tomography when it acquired error bars and dose budgets. For world-model discovery — reading a causal model out of an agent's near-optimal behavior — the inversion step is a theorem of Richens and Everitt [RE24]: any policy that stays near-optimal across a rich family of interventions on its environment determines an approximate causal model of that environment."}, {"id": "MAIS-O36", "n": 36, "title": "Global ℓ¹ dictionary recovery under independent supports", "file": "MAIS-O36.md", "safety": ["interpretability"], "math": ["probability", "optimization", "statistics"], "stars": 3, "empirical": false, "resolved": false, "agenda": "A3", "short": "When a sparse autoencoder converges, is the dictionary it finds the one that generated the data? The known guarantee for the $\\ell^1$-penalized estimator is local: Gribonval, Jenatton, and Bach [GJB15] proved that a minimum exists near the true dictionary, leaving open whether a merged or rotated dictionary far away scores even better."}, {"id": "MAIS-O37", "n": 37, "title": "Two smeared features: recover, merge, or split", "file": "MAIS-O37.md", "safety": ["interpretability"], "math": ["optimization", "probability", "convex geometry"], "stars": 3, "empirical": false, "resolved": false, "agenda": "A3", "short": "When a sparse autoencoder reports several atoms for one concept, has it failed, or has it faithfully tiled a feature that was never a single direction? Practitioners call the phenomenon feature splitting (Chanin et al. [CWDB+24]), but in the standard generative model a feature is one direction, so splitting cannot even be stated there."}, {"id": "MAIS-O38", "n": 38, "title": "Polynomial sample complexity for dictionary uniqueness with growing sparsity", "file": "MAIS-O38.md", "safety": ["interpretability"], "math": ["combinatorics", "statistics"], "stars": 3, "empirical": false, "resolved": false, "agenda": "A3", "short": "How many samples must a dictionary-learning dataset contain before only one dictionary can explain it? If the concepts read out of a network are to be trusted, the factorization behind them should be pinned down by the data. The classical uniqueness theorems answer with sample counts containing $\\binom{m}{k}$ — polynomial in the number of features $m$ for fixed sparsity $k$, but exploding as soon as $k$ grows with $m$, which is the regime superposition lives in."}, {"id": "MAIS-O39", "n": 39, "title": "Does the amortized encoder change the learned dictionary?", "file": "MAIS-O39.md", "safety": ["interpretability"], "math": ["optimization", "statistics"], "stars": 3, "empirical": false, "resolved": false, "agenda": "A3", "short": "A sparse autoencoder never solves the sparse-coding program it is named for. Instead of computing the optimal code for each data point, it applies one learned affine-plus-ReLU map — cheap, but not optimal. O'Neill, Gumran, and Klindt [OGK25] proved this shortcut can produce inaccurate codes; whether it moves the minimizing dictionary, the object interpretability actually reads off, is open."}, {"id": "MAIS-O40", "n": 40, "title": "Regular pentagon as global minimizer of a ReLU autoencoder", "file": "MAIS-O40.md", "safety": ["interpretability"], "math": ["optimization", "harmonic analysis"], "stars": 3, "empirical": false, "resolved": false, "agenda": "A3", "short": "Train a tiny autoencoder to squeeze five sparse features through two dimensions, and its five weight vectors crystallize into a regular pentagon. This is the experiment of Toy models of superposition (Elhage et al. [EHOS+22]) in which superposition — a network representing more features than it has dimensions, a central obstacle to reading off what a model has learned — was first exhibited, and the pentagon is its emblem. No theorem yet says the pentagon wins."}, {"id": "MAIS-O41", "n": 41, "title": "Two-feature phase diagram for ℓ¹ dictionary learning", "file": "MAIS-O41.md", "safety": ["interpretability"], "math": ["optimization", "convex geometry"], "stars": 1, "empirical": false, "resolved": false, "agenda": "A3", "short": "A sparse autoencoder is interpretability's tool for extracting the concept directions a network stores in superposition; whether it recovers the true directions or blends co-occurring ones into a single reported \"feature\" is what decides if the extracted concepts can be trusted. Two features suffice to see it both fail and succeed."}, {"id": "MAIS-O42", "n": 42, "title": "Pentagon optimality for a pure ReLU packing energy", "file": "MAIS-O42.md", "safety": ["interpretability"], "math": ["optimization", "harmonic analysis"], "stars": 2, "empirical": false, "resolved": false, "agenda": "A3", "short": "Five points on or inside a circle, repelling one another through a ReLU: prove the regular pentagon wins. This is the pentagon conjecture for the superposition toy model (MAIS-O40) with the interaction between sparsity levels stripped away, leaving a pure packing energy."}, {"id": "MAIS-O43", "n": 43, "title": "Measure the sparse-autoencoder recovery–merging phase diagram", "file": "MAIS-O43.md", "safety": ["interpretability"], "math": ["computational", "statistics"], "stars": 1, "empirical": true, "resolved": false, "agenda": "A3", "short": "Where, quantitatively, does a sparse autoencoder stop recovering features and start absorbing them? The theory of that boundary is open (MAIS-O3, MAIS-O36); this problem specifies an experiment — synthetic data with a tunable fraction of nested feature pairs — that measures it first. The protocol pins down sample counts, optimizer, and thresholds, and requires publishing seeds and checkpoints."}, {"id": "MAIS-O44", "n": 44, "title": "Does penalizing average interference lower coherence?", "file": "MAIS-O44.md", "safety": ["interpretability"], "math": ["optimization", "harmonic analysis", "probability"], "stars": 2, "empirical": false, "resolved": false, "agenda": "A4", "short": "That a regularizer reduces the penalized quantity is a two-line fact about real numbers, true for every loss and every penalty. The mathematical content of \"regularize for interpretability\" lies in the distance between the penalty available to gradient-based training — a smooth average — and the quantity the interpreter cares about, a max."}, {"id": "MAIS-O45", "n": 45, "title": "Does penalizing interference make features recoverable by dictionary learning?", "file": "MAIS-O45.md", "safety": ["interpretability"], "math": ["statistics", "optimization", "probability"], "stars": 2, "empirical": false, "resolved": false, "agenda": "A4", "short": "Coherence, the worst-case overlap between stored features, is a property of the weights; the post-hoc interpreter never sees the weights. She sees only activations, and runs a dictionary-learning estimator on them. Does training with an interference penalty actually help her?"}, {"id": "MAIS-O46", "n": 46, "title": "Does lower coherence imply better dictionary recovery?", "file": "MAIS-O46.md", "safety": ["interpretability"], "math": ["statistics", "harmonic analysis"], "stars": 2, "empirical": false, "resolved": false, "agenda": "A4", "short": "An antipodal pair of stored features has coherence $1$ — the worst possible score on the standard legibility metric — yet a sparse autoencoder with nonnegative codes treats $W_i$ and $-W_i$ as different atoms, so the pair is plausibly recoverable. Is coherence ranking dictionaries in the wrong order?"}, {"id": "MAIS-O47", "n": 47, "title": "Uniqueness of ReLU toy-model minimizers up to symmetry", "file": "MAIS-O47.md", "safety": ["interpretability"], "math": ["optimization", "convex geometry"], "stars": 3, "empirical": false, "resolved": false, "agenda": "A4", "short": "If two independent training runs find genuinely different optima, two auditors will report different features. Stability of interpretation is thus a uniqueness question: is the optimum a single orbit of the problem's symmetries?"}, {"id": "MAIS-O48", "n": 48, "title": "Loss frontier for neuron-feature alignment under a bottleneck", "file": "MAIS-O48.md", "safety": ["interpretability"], "math": ["optimization", "probability"], "stars": 2, "empirical": false, "resolved": false, "agenda": "A4", "short": "A hidden neuron that reads a single feature can be labeled; one that mixes several cannot. What does it cost, in task loss, to demand that every neuron be almost pure — and does the first little bit of impurity buy anything at all?"}, {"id": "MAIS-O49", "n": 49, "title": "Exact cost of weight sparsity in the ReLU toy model", "file": "MAIS-O49.md", "safety": ["interpretability"], "math": ["optimization", "probability", "combinatorics"], "stars": 2, "empirical": false, "resolved": false, "agenda": "A4", "short": "A network in which each feature is wired to a single neuron is legible by inspection: every activation coordinate is an explicit signed combination of named features. Gao et al. [GRCG+25] found empirically that training transformers with almost all weights zeroed buys interpretable circuits at a measured capability cost. In the toy model of superposition of Elhage et al. [EHOS+22], that cost can be asked for exactly."}, {"id": "MAIS-O50", "n": 50, "title": "Which minima does gradient flow select from random initialization?", "file": "MAIS-O50.md", "safety": ["interpretability", "generalization"], "math": ["dynamical systems", "probability", "optimization"], "stars": 3, "empirical": false, "resolved": false, "agenda": "A4", "short": "When a task has both a legible optimum and a scrambled one at the same loss, the network we get is decided not by minimization but by the optimizer; whether we can read the internals of the model we deploy hangs on that choice. What law does gradient flow from random weights induce on the set of minima?"}, {"id": "MAIS-O51", "n": 51, "title": "The regularized phase diagram of the smallest model", "file": "MAIS-O51.md", "safety": ["interpretability"], "math": ["optimization", "probability"], "stars": 1, "empirical": false, "resolved": false, "agenda": "A4", "short": "Two features, one dimension: the smallest network that must choose what to forget. Elhage et al. [EHOS+22] solved the unregularized case; nobody has analyzed it with the interference penalty that \"training for interpretability\" would actually add."}, {"id": "MAIS-O52", "n": 52, "title": "How many Fourier frequencies do modular-addition networks use?", "file": "MAIS-O52.md", "safety": ["interpretability"], "math": ["probability", "dynamical systems", "harmonic analysis"], "stars": 3, "empirical": false, "resolved": false, "agenda": "A5", "short": "A network trained on addition mod 113 expressed its outputs through five of the 56 available Fourier frequencies. Yet Morwani, Edelman, Oncescu, Zhao, and Kakade [MEOZK24] proved that for a quadratic activation, the networks maximizing the margin in the $\\ell_{2,3}$ norm adapted to the architecture use all the frequencies, one per neuron. Sparse or dense: which regime does actual training occupy as the modulus grows?"}, {"id": "MAIS-O53", "n": 53, "title": "Law of large numbers for neuron representation multiplicities", "file": "MAIS-O53.md", "safety": ["interpretability"], "math": ["probability", "representation theory", "dynamical systems"], "stars": 3, "empirical": false, "resolved": false, "agenda": "A5", "short": "In networks trained on group multiplication, individual neurons commit: in Nanda et al.'s mod-113 network [NCLSS23], 84.6% of neurons have at least 85% of their weight energy on a single Fourier frequency, and Chughtai, Chan, and Nanda's nonabelian networks [CCN23] cluster neuron-by-neuron around single irreducible representations. Circuit-level interpretability implicitly bets that this commitment is lawful: reading one network should tell you about the next."}, {"id": "MAIS-O54", "n": 54, "title": "Are selection probabilities proportional to squared representation dimension?", "file": "MAIS-O54.md", "safety": ["interpretability"], "math": ["representation theory", "probability"], "stars": 3, "empirical": false, "resolved": false, "agenda": "A5", "short": "A Gaussian random function on a finite group $G$ puts mean squared mass $d_\\rho^2/|G|$ in the isotypic component of each irreducible representation $\\rho$ of dimension $d_\\rho$ — the Artin–Wedderburn block sizes. If each neuron of a trained network is won by its initially heaviest component, the fraction of neurons landing on each representation should be proportional to $d_\\rho^2$. Is it?"}, {"id": "MAIS-O55", "n": 55, "title": "Neuron purity and representation selection for S_3 networks", "file": "MAIS-O55.md", "safety": ["interpretability"], "math": ["dynamical systems", "representation theory", "probability"], "stars": 3, "empirical": false, "resolved": false, "agenda": "A5", "short": "Mechanistic interpretability leans on universality: a circuit reverse-engineered in one trained network repays the labor only if other runs learn it too. For networks trained to multiply in a finite group, Chughtai, Chan, and Nanda [CCN23] found the evidence mixed: every run learns irreducible representations of the group, but which ones varies with the seed."}, {"id": "MAIS-O56", "n": 56, "title": "Vanishing weight decay selects both S_3 representations", "file": "MAIS-O56.md", "safety": ["interpretability"], "math": ["dynamical systems", "representation theory", "optimization"], "stars": 3, "empirical": false, "resolved": false, "agenda": "A5", "short": "How far a mechanistic interpretation of one trained network carries to another depends on whether training selects the same circuits each time. For networks trained on group multiplication, Chughtai, Chan, and Nanda [CCN23] found universality in the weak sense only: the trained circuits are built from irreducible characters of the group, but which irreducibles appear varies from one random seed to the next."}, {"id": "MAIS-O57", "n": 57, "title": "Does gradient flow on S_5 select randomly among representation sets?", "file": "MAIS-O57.md", "safety": ["interpretability"], "math": ["probability", "dynamical systems", "representation theory"], "stars": 3, "empirical": false, "resolved": false, "agenda": "A5", "short": "Mechanistic interpretability hopes that circuits are universal: that independently trained networks converge on the same internal mechanism, so understanding reverse-engineered from one model transfers to the next."}, {"id": "MAIS-O58", "n": 58, "title": "Exchangeability of learned frequencies beyond multiplicative symmetry", "file": "MAIS-O58.md", "safety": ["interpretability"], "math": ["probability", "harmonic analysis", "dynamical systems"], "stars": 3, "empirical": false, "resolved": false, "agenda": "A5", "short": "When a network learns addition mod $p$, it expresses its outputs through a sparse random set of the $(p-1)/2$ Fourier frequencies, a phenomenon reverse-engineered by Nanda, Chan, Lieberum, Smith, and Steinhardt [NCLSS23] and extended to general finite groups by Chughtai, Chan, and Nanda [CCN23], whose seed tables show the set varying from one initialization to the next."}, {"id": "MAIS-O59", "n": 59, "title": "Two neurons learning mod-5 addition: which frequencies win?", "file": "MAIS-O59.md", "safety": ["interpretability"], "math": ["dynamical systems", "probability", "harmonic analysis"], "stars": 2, "empirical": false, "resolved": false, "agenda": "A5", "short": "Addition mod 5 has two Fourier frequencies; give the network exactly two quadratic neurons, and the competition between representations for a finite supply of neurons appears in its smallest form. The whole system is an explicit polynomial ODE on $\\mathbb{R}^{30}$ with Gaussian initial data — small enough to attack directly, and the smallest open case of the headline selection problem MAIS-O5."}, {"id": "MAIS-O60", "n": 60, "title": "Does a single ReLU neuron align to one frequency?", "file": "MAIS-O60.md", "safety": ["interpretability"], "math": ["dynamical systems", "probability", "harmonic analysis"], "stars": 2, "empirical": false, "resolved": true, "agenda": "A5", "short": "Strip the network down to a single rectifier neuron on addition mod $p$. Trained wide networks on this task end up with neurons each committed to one Fourier frequency, as Nanda, Chan, Lieberum, Smith, and Steinhardt observed [NCLSS23]; here the question is isolated in one neuron: started from a unit-scale Gaussian, does its direction converge, and is the limit a single frequency?"}, {"id": "MAIS-O61", "n": 61, "title": "Pilot measurement of representation selection in small trained networks", "file": "MAIS-O61.md", "safety": ["interpretability"], "math": ["computational", "statistics"], "stars": 1, "empirical": true, "resolved": false, "agenda": "A5", "short": "Which irreducible representations a trained network uses varies by random seed (Nanda, Chan, Lieberum, Smith, and Steinhardt [NCLSS23]; Chughtai, Chan, and Nanda [CCN23]), and the published data on that variation amount to a handful of seeds at a couple of configurations. Before anyone proves a selection theorem — or even conjectures the right constants — someone should measure the law."}, {"id": "MAIS-O62", "n": 62, "title": "Minimal network width for exact modular addition", "file": "MAIS-O62.md", "safety": ["interpretability", "generalization"], "math": ["complexity theory", "algebraic geometry", "harmonic analysis"], "stars": 2, "empirical": false, "resolved": false, "agenda": "A6", "short": "How many hidden neurons does a quadratic network need to compute addition mod $p$ exactly? The answer sets the stage for every geometric question about this model: below the minimal width, the singular variety of exact fits is empty, so the learning coefficients that MAIS-O6 asks about (the volume-growth exponents from singular learning theory that govern generalization and which exact fits Bayesian learning favors) have nothing to measure."}, {"id": "MAIS-O63", "n": 63, "title": "Learning coefficients of modular addition", "file": "MAIS-O63.md", "safety": ["interpretability", "generalization"], "math": ["algebraic geometry", "statistics"], "stars": 3, "empirical": false, "resolved": false, "agenda": "A6", "short": "Watanabe's singular learning theory [W09] predicts where a Bayesian learner's posterior settles: on the parameters of smallest local learning coefficient $\\lambda(w)$, a volume-growth exponent — the volume of parameters near $w$ with population loss at most $\\varepsilon$ scales like $\\varepsilon^{\\lambda(w)}$ times $(\\log\\frac{1}{\\varepsilon})^{m(w)-1}$, where the integer $m(w)$ is the multiplicity."}, {"id": "MAIS-O64", "n": 64, "title": "Marginal cost of width for modular-addition networks", "file": "MAIS-O64.md", "safety": ["interpretability", "generalization"], "math": ["algebraic geometry", "statistics"], "stars": 3, "empirical": false, "resolved": false, "agenda": "A6", "short": "What does one idle neuron cost, as learning theory counts cost? The ledger here is Watanabe's singular learning theory [W09], which prices a solution by the geometry of the loss near it rather than by counting parameters."}, {"id": "MAIS-O65", "n": 65, "title": "Learning coefficients of modular addition under cross-entropy", "file": "MAIS-O65.md", "safety": ["interpretability", "generalization"], "math": ["algebraic geometry", "statistics"], "stars": 3, "empirical": false, "resolved": false, "agenda": "A6", "short": "Singular learning theory predicts which algorithm a trained network implements before training it — provided its learning coefficients can be computed for the training setup actually used (MAIS-O63). Practice trains modular addition with softmax and cross-entropy, not squared error — but a deterministic label law is not realizable by any softmax model at finite parameters, so the realizable singular learning theory of Watanabe [W09] says nothing until the truth is smoothed."}, {"id": "MAIS-O66", "n": 66, "title": "Generic constancy of learning coefficients over attention teachers", "file": "MAIS-O66.md", "safety": ["interpretability", "generalization"], "math": ["algebraic geometry", "statistics"], "stars": 3, "empirical": false, "resolved": false, "agenda": "A6", "short": "The local learning coefficient, in the pointwise sense of Lau, Furman, Wang, Murfet, and Wei [LFWMW23], is the complexity measure that developmental interpretability estimates inside trained networks to watch structure form during training. In Watanabe's singular learning theory [W09] it replaces the parameter count: for a localized prior it sets the complexity penalty in the Bayes free energy and the $\\lambda/n$ decay of the generalization error."}, {"id": "MAIS-O67", "n": 67, "title": "Learning coefficients of one-head softmax attention", "file": "MAIS-O67.md", "safety": ["interpretability", "generalization"], "math": ["algebraic geometry", "statistics"], "stars": 3, "empirical": false, "resolved": false, "agenda": "A6", "short": "Exact learning coefficients are known for linear networks of every depth, but for attention — the layer the transformer is built from — not a single teacher-student value has been computed. This problem asks for the first one: the attention analog of the Aoyagi–Watanabe formula [AW05], in which the coefficient of reduced-rank regression depends on the truth only through its rank."}, {"id": "MAIS-O68", "n": 68, "title": "Learning coefficients of linear attention", "file": "MAIS-O68.md", "safety": ["interpretability", "generalization"], "math": ["algebraic geometry", "statistics", "computational"], "stars": 2, "empirical": false, "resolved": false, "agenda": "A6", "short": "Singular learning theory is the mathematical backbone of developmental interpretability: the learning coefficient $\\lambda$ of a network's loss landscape replaces the parameter count in the asymptotics of Bayesian generalization, and estimating it numerically is how that program detects phase transitions in training."}, {"id": "MAIS-O69", "n": 69, "title": "When does the localized posterior estimate the local learning coefficient?", "file": "MAIS-O69.md", "safety": ["interpretability", "generalization"], "math": ["statistics", "probability"], "stars": 2, "empirical": false, "resolved": false, "agenda": "A6", "short": "Local learning coefficients are estimated in practice by sampling from a posterior pinned near the point of interest — inside networks with billions of parameters. But the theorem underpinning the estimator cuts the wrong way: with any fixed localization strength, the Gaussian pin is just another smooth positive prior, so the estimator converges to the global coefficient, not necessarily the local one. How fast must the pin tighten with the sample size?"}, {"id": "MAIS-O70", "n": 70, "title": "Local learning coefficients of reduced-rank regression", "file": "MAIS-O70.md", "safety": ["interpretability", "generalization"], "math": ["algebraic geometry", "statistics"], "stars": 2, "empirical": false, "resolved": false, "agenda": "A6", "short": "The one singular model family solved completely — reduced-rank regression — was solved globally: Aoyagi and Watanabe [AW05] computed the minimum of the local learning coefficient over the whole set of exact fits, as a closed form in four integers. But the estimators now used on neural networks, introduced by Lau, Furman, Wang, Murfet, and Wei [LFWMW23], are local, evaluated at a point, and the pointwise values on this benchmark family have never been tabulated."}, {"id": "MAIS-O71", "n": 71, "title": "Free energy asymptotics for continuous-input ReLU networks", "file": "MAIS-O71.md", "safety": ["interpretability", "generalization"], "math": ["algebraic geometry", "probability", "statistics"], "stars": 3, "empirical": false, "resolved": false, "agenda": "A6", "short": "Watanabe's free energy expansion [W09] — the theorem that makes the learning coefficient govern generalization — assumes the population loss is real-analytic. The ReLU activation $\\max(t,0)$ is not, and the standard workaround fails outside toy settings: on a finite input set the parameter space splits into finitely many cells where the loss is polynomial, but for a continuous input distribution there is no finite activation partition. Does the expansion survive?"}, {"id": "MAIS-O72", "n": 72, "title": "Finiteness and frontier condition for learning-coefficient strata", "file": "MAIS-O72.md", "safety": ["generalization"], "math": ["algebraic geometry"], "stars": 3, "empirical": false, "resolved": false, "agenda": "A7", "short": "Singular learning theory predicts that Bayesian learning selects, among the zero-loss parameters of a network, the flattest ones — those of smallest learning coefficient. Any theorem about \"effective dynamics on the strata\" of the optimal set presupposes that the level sets of the learning coefficient are honest geometric objects. Are they?"}, {"id": "MAIS-O73", "n": 73, "title": "Eyring–Kramers law for singular wells", "file": "MAIS-O73.md", "safety": ["generalization"], "math": ["probability", "algebraic geometry"], "stars": 3, "empirical": false, "resolved": false, "agenda": "A7", "short": "How long does noisy training take to escape a flat valley? For a Morse landscape the classical Eyring–Kramers formula answers with a constant prefactor times $e^{h/\\varepsilon}$, where $h$ is the barrier height."}, {"id": "MAIS-O74", "n": 74, "title": "Eyring–Kramers prefactor for a singular well and Morse gate", "file": "MAIS-O74.md", "safety": ["generalization"], "math": ["probability", "algebraic geometry"], "stars": 2, "empirical": false, "resolved": false, "agenda": "A7", "short": "Take a double-well landscape where one well is not a point but a singular set, and the pass between the wells is an ordinary Morse saddle. The classical Eyring–Kramers formula gives the mean crossing time with a constant prefactor; this conjecture says the singular well contributes a power of the temperature, with exponent read off from Watanabe's learning coefficient [W09]."}, {"id": "MAIS-O75", "n": 75, "title": "Entropic selection between two flat strata on the torus", "file": "MAIS-O75.md", "safety": ["generalization"], "math": ["probability"], "stars": 2, "empirical": false, "resolved": false, "agenda": "A7", "short": "Metastability concerns wells separated by loss barriers, crossed on timescales exponential in the inverse temperature by the Eyring–Kramers law of Bovier, Eckhoff, Gayrard, and Klein [BEGK04]."}, {"id": "MAIS-O76", "n": 76, "title": "SGD limit diffusions on a singular set of minimizers", "file": "MAIS-O76.md", "safety": ["generalization"], "math": ["probability", "algebraic geometry"], "stars": 3, "empirical": false, "resolved": false, "agenda": "A7", "short": "What does stochastic gradient descent do after it reaches zero loss? For an overparametrized model the zero-loss parameters form a whole set, typically singular, and which point on it training selects is the implicit bias that determines what the trained model generalizes to."}, {"id": "MAIS-O77", "n": 77, "title": "Learning coefficients on the matrix factorization fiber and saddles", "file": "MAIS-O77.md", "safety": ["generalization"], "math": ["algebraic geometry"], "stars": 2, "empirical": false, "resolved": false, "agenda": "A7", "short": "The two-layer linear network $x \\mapsto BAx$, with $A \\in \\mathbb{R}^{H \\times M}$, $B \\in \\mathbb{R}^{N \\times H}$, and population loss $L(A,B) = \\tfrac12 \\Vert  BA - \\Phi \\Vert _F^2$ against a rank-$r$ target $\\Phi$, is the one model where both the Bayesian and the dynamical accounts of training are explicit."}, {"id": "MAIS-O78", "n": 78, "title": "A time-sample correspondence for the deep linear network", "file": "MAIS-O78.md", "safety": ["generalization"], "math": ["statistics", "probability"], "stars": 3, "empirical": false, "resolved": false, "agenda": "A7", "short": "Two staircases describe learning in the two-layer linear network $L(A,B) = \\tfrac12 \\Vert  BA - \\Phi \\Vert _F^2$ (weights $A \\in \\mathbb{R}^{H \\times M}$ and $B \\in \\mathbb{R}^{N \\times H}$ with hidden width $H$; target $\\Phi \\in \\mathbb{R}^{N \\times M}$ of rank $r \\le H$ with distinct singular values $s_1 > \\dots > s_r > 0$), and they are graded by different variables."}, {"id": "MAIS-O79", "n": 79, "title": "Saddles as effective minima under coarse-graining", "file": "MAIS-O79.md", "safety": ["generalization"], "math": ["dynamical systems", "algebraic geometry"], "stars": 3, "empirical": false, "resolved": false, "agenda": "A7", "short": "Can a training plateau turn a saddle into a local minimum at the resolution visible to the dynamics? Researchers who study how a network's internal structure emerges during training say that during a plateau the saddle is a local minimum of an effective loss. Here is one way to make the slogan precise. For $\\varepsilon, \\delta > 0$ define the effective loss"}, {"id": "MAIS-O80", "n": 80, "title": "Is leap complexity determined by singularity data?", "file": "MAIS-O80.md", "safety": ["generalization"], "math": ["algebraic geometry", "combinatorics"], "stars": 3, "empirical": false, "resolved": false, "agenda": "A7", "short": "Two bookkeepings claim to govern stagewise learning, and they live on different objects. The safety interest is developmental interpretability's: stagewise training is where new capabilities arrive abruptly, and the hope is to predict a run's stages from geometric quantities measurable along the way."}, {"id": "MAIS-O81", "n": 81, "title": "Does a saddle have a well-defined free energy?", "file": "MAIS-O81.md", "safety": ["generalization"], "math": ["statistics", "algebraic geometry"], "stars": 3, "empirical": false, "resolved": false, "agenda": "A7", "short": "Developmental interpretability reads a training run as a descent down a staircase of saddles and prices each rung by the Bayesian free-energy ladder of singular learning theory: Watanabe's asymptotics [W09], localized to regions at positive loss by Lau, Furman, Wang, Murfet, and Wei [LFWMW23], assign a region around a critical set at loss $L_C$ with learning coefficient $\\lambda_C$ the free energy $n L_C + \\lambda_C \\log n$ at sample size $n$."}, {"id": "MAIS-O82", "n": 82, "title": "Wide networks select the proxy policy with probability one", "file": "MAIS-O82.md", "safety": ["generalization"], "math": ["probability", "optimization"], "stars": 3, "empirical": false, "resolved": false, "agenda": "A8", "short": "An agent trained to collect a coin that always sat at the right end of its levels learned \"move right,\" not \"get the coin\" — two rules that agree on every training level and disagree the moment the coin moves. Predicting which of two training-indistinguishable policies gradient descent selects is the mathematical core of goal misgeneralization."}, {"id": "MAIS-O83", "n": 83, "title": "Does some finite-width network favor the intended goal?", "file": "MAIS-O83.md", "safety": ["generalization"], "math": ["probability", "optimization"], "stars": 3, "empirical": false, "resolved": false, "agenda": "A8", "short": "Both infinite-width limits of the coin-line network walk away from a displaced coin: the kernel flow provably drives the off-distribution probe logit to $+\\infty$, and the mean-field limit — the margin maximizer identified by Chizat and Bach [CB20], granted weak convergence of the representing measures — is strictly positive there. Does anything at finite width prefer to collect the coin instead?"}, {"id": "MAIS-O84", "n": 84, "title": "The crossover time and its asymptotics", "file": "MAIS-O84.md", "safety": ["generalization"], "math": ["dynamical systems", "optimization", "probability"], "stars": 3, "empirical": false, "resolved": false, "agenda": "A8", "short": "Randomizing the coin's position in as little as 2% of training levels largely cured the coin collector of Langosco et al. [LKSP+22] of running past a displaced coin. Why 2%? In the max-margin theory the diversity level $\\varepsilon$ is absent from the endpoint: for every $\\varepsilon>0$ the limiting linear classifier is the intended goal. The empirical phenomenon must therefore live at finite time — in how long the rare corrective examples take to overturn the proxy."}, {"id": "MAIS-O85", "n": 85, "title": "Which feature maps make the max-margin classifier misgeneralize?", "file": "MAIS-O85.md", "safety": ["generalization"], "math": ["convex geometry", "optimization"], "stars": 2, "empirical": false, "resolved": false, "agenda": "A8", "short": "On the coin line at zero training diversity, the encoding decides: linear gradient descent on the features $(1,p,c)$ provably learns \"move right,\" while on $(1,c-p)$ it provably learns to approach the coin. One-hot features (indicator vectors of the two positions, concatenated) and every monomial encoding $\\psi_k(p,c)=(p^ic^j)_{i+j\\le k}$ side with the proxy. What property of a feature map is responsible?"}, {"id": "MAIS-O86", "n": 86, "title": "Does gradient descent match the Gaussian process posterior?", "file": "MAIS-O86.md", "safety": ["generalization"], "math": ["probability", "statistics"], "stars": 3, "empirical": false, "resolved": false, "agenda": "A8", "short": "Two candidate theories predict what a trained network does off-distribution: the implicit bias of gradient descent, and the Bayesian posterior of the infinite-width Gaussian process prior. On the coin line both make a prediction about the same single number — the probability that the trained agent walks away from a displaced coin — so the theories can be made to disagree, or agree, in a box."}, {"id": "MAIS-O87", "n": 87, "title": "Does policy gradient stay misgeneralized despite training diversity?", "file": "MAIS-O87.md", "safety": ["generalization"], "math": ["dynamical systems", "probability"], "stars": 2, "empirical": false, "resolved": false, "agenda": "A8", "short": "The coin problem is the agenda's one-dimensional version of the coin-collector experiment of Langosco, Koch, Sharkey, Pfau, Orseau, and Krueger [LKSP+22], in which an agent trained only on right-lying coins learned \"move right\" rather than \"get the coin\". Reinforcement learning changes the coin problem in one essential way: the training data become endogenous, since the states an agent experiences depend on the policy it currently has."}, {"id": "MAIS-O88", "n": 88, "title": "Misgeneralization versus diversity for reinforcement learning", "file": "MAIS-O88.md", "safety": ["generalization"], "math": ["probability", "dynamical systems"], "stars": 3, "empirical": false, "resolved": false, "agenda": "A8", "short": "Langosco et al. [LKSP+22] plotted how often their trained coin collector ignores a displaced coin against the percentage of training levels with a randomized coin, and found the failure rate falls steeply: about 2% diversity largely restores the intended behavior. The coin line is small enough to ask for that curve as a theorem."}, {"id": "MAIS-O89", "n": 89, "title": "Boundary-state residual of max-margin gradient descent", "file": "MAIS-O89.md", "safety": ["generalization"], "math": ["optimization", "dynamical systems"], "stars": 1, "empirical": false, "resolved": false, "agenda": "A8", "short": "By the implicit-bias theorem of Soudry, Hoffer, Nacson, Gunasekar, and Srebro [SHNGS18], gradient descent on separable data converges in direction to the max-margin classifier. That direction predicts a trained linear classifier only to leading order, and the bounded residual it leaves behind is usually beneath notice. On the coin line — the agenda's one-dimensional toy of goal misgeneralization — the residual controls half the test return."}, {"id": "MAIS-O90", "n": 90, "title": "Does finite-width training inherit the kernel flow's selection?", "file": "MAIS-O90.md", "safety": ["generalization"], "math": ["probability", "optimization"], "stars": 2, "empirical": false, "resolved": false, "agenda": "A8", "short": "The coin line is the agenda's minimal model of goal misgeneralization: trained only on states with the coin at the right end of the line, does the network learn the proxy rule \"move right\" or the intended rule \"go to the coin\"? The two rules agree on every training state and disagree at an off-distribution probe state with the coin displaced to the left end."}, {"id": "MAIS-O91", "n": 91, "title": "Numerical atlas of misgeneralization across widths and diversity", "file": "MAIS-O91.md", "safety": ["generalization"], "math": ["computational", "probability"], "stars": 1, "empirical": true, "resolved": false, "agenda": "A8", "short": "Every open problem in the coin-line agenda asks for a number a laptop can estimate: the probability, over random initialization, that a trained policy walks away from a displaced coin. Nobody has computed the table."}, {"id": "MAIS-O92", "n": 92, "title": "The outcome law of one rectifier neuron", "file": "MAIS-O92.md", "safety": ["interpretability"], "math": ["dynamical systems", "probability", "harmonic analysis"], "stars": 3, "empirical": false, "resolved": false, "agenda": "A5", "short": "A single ReLU neuron trained on addition mod $p$ can end in at least three ways: it can die, freezing at a spectrally flat state; it can survive, memorize one entry of the addition table, and diverge with the same flat spectrum; or it can align to a single Fourier frequency, the monosemantic outcome that wide trained networks exhibit neuron by neuron."}], "agendas": [{"id": "MAIS-A1", "expands": "MAIS-O1", "home": "Logic and proof complexity", "title": "Quantitative bounded Löb", "status": "Draft · July 12, 2026", "short": "Löb's theorem converts a proof of \"if $P$ is provable then $P$\" into a proof of $P$ outright, by an explicit construction with an explicit cost."}, {"id": "MAIS-A2", "expands": "MAIS-O2", "home": "Probability, causality, and statistics", "title": "Recovering world-models from behavior", "status": "Draft · July 12, 2026", "short": "Two theorems of Richens and coauthors show that any policy near-optimal across a rich family of interventions determines an approximate causal model of its environment — but the extraction consumes unlimited noiseless queries. This agenda develops the finite-sample side: explicit margin conditions replacing measure-zero genericity, query complexity under exact, sampled, and Boltzmann-perturbed answers, and the error floor imposed by the agent's regret."}, {"id": "MAIS-A3", "expands": "MAIS-O3", "home": "Sparse recovery and geometry", "title": "The geometry and identifiability of superposition", "status": "Draft · July 12, 2026", "short": "A neural network can store many more concepts than it has dimensions, as nearly orthogonal directions read out through sparse coefficients; the recovery tool used in practice is an $\\ell^1$-penalized dictionary learner. Classical dictionary-learning theory assumes independent supports, but real concepts co-occur. Two small propositions bracket the problem: nested supports force merging at small positive penalty, while solo firing restores recovery in the zero-penalty constrained limit."}, {"id": "MAIS-A4", "expands": "MAIS-O4", "home": "Frame theory and optimization", "title": "Training for interpretability", "status": "Draft · July 12, 2026", "short": "Can a training objective make a network's internal features easy to recover? \"Easy to interpret\" is not yet a mathematical property, and this agenda does not pretend it is: it isolates three precise surrogates — coherence, $\\ell^1$-recoverability, monosemanticity — inside a single toy model, proves small theorems marking the boundary of what is easy, and poses the problems that remain where resources are scarce and training means dynamics rather than exact minimization."}, {"id": "MAIS-A5", "expands": "MAIS-O5", "home": "Representation theory and dynamics", "title": "Which irreducible representations does training select?", "status": "Draft · July 12, 2026", "short": "Small networks trained to multiply in a finite group $G$ discover representation-theoretic algorithms, but which irreducible representations appear varies with the random initialization, and no theorem predicts the variation. This agenda defines the training ensemble and a black-box observable — the key set of irreducible representations visible in a trained network's outputs — precisely enough that the question becomes the law of a random subset of $\\mathrm{Irr}(G)$."}, {"id": "MAIS-A6", "expands": "MAIS-O6", "home": "Singular learning and algebraic geometry", "title": "Does geometric simplicity force a legible mechanism?", "status": "Draft · July 12, 2026", "short": "For a quadratic network computing addition mod $p$, the population loss is an explicit degree-six polynomial, and Fourier inversion gives exact fits built from single-frequency neurons — the algorithm found by reverse-engineering trained networks. The headline question: in a fixed ball, is every exact fit of smallest local learning coefficient of this single-frequency form?"}, {"id": "MAIS-A7", "expands": "MAIS-O7", "home": "Stochastic dynamics and singular geometry", "title": "Effective loss and learning dynamics", "status": "Draft · July 13, 2026", "short": "Watanabe's free-energy asymptotics predict which solutions Bayesian learning prefers as sample size grows. Gradient descent is not Bayesian, but in solvable deep-linear models it follows a temporal staircase, learning structures one at a time."}, {"id": "MAIS-A8", "expands": "MAIS-O8", "home": "Optimization and learning theory", "title": "A predictive theory of out-of-distribution generalization", "status": "Draft · July 16, 2026", "short": "An agent trained to collect a coin that always sat at the right end of its levels learned \"move right,\" not \"get the coin.\" This agenda turns that episode into mathematics: a one-dimensional environment with the degeneracy built in — two policies indistinguishable on the training distribution. The linear case and several infinite-width limits can be solved; the finite-width selection law, its rare-diversity crossover, and the boundary between proxy and goal generalization remain open."}]};